; io, DECLARED BY HAND — the sorts, and the two method sets a hand file has read.
;
; A module in a target is a signature Σ = (S, Ω): its sorts and the operations
; typed over them. These three are S for `io`, and they live here because a type
; has ONE OWNER — `encoding/hex` declared them until 2026-09-16, which recorded no
; dependency and let a second file declare `io.Writer` to mean something else
; (declaration-surface.md §4.2, theories.md §3.2).
;
; Ω IS DECLARED IN A COMPANION: the child module sharing a type's name holds the
; operations on it (theories.md §3.3). That is the only way an INTERFACE gets a
; method set, and its absence was the wall hex recorded — a value of
; `io.WriteCloser` could be written to and never closed, so the acceptance
; program's dump was missing exactly what `Close` would write (hex-2026-09-14 §6).
;
; Every claim below was read off src/io/io.go in the Go 1.27 toolchain.

(target go
  (module go/io
    (type Reader      (host "io.Reader"))
    (type Writer      (host "io.Writer"))
    (type Closer      (host "io.Closer"))
    (type WriteCloser (host "io.WriteCloser"))

    ; EACH METHOD SET IS WRITTEN INSIDE THE MODULE THAT OWNS THE TYPE. A child
    ; module's path is its parent's followed by its own, so `Writer` here is
    ; `go/io/Writer` (spec/target-files.md §1a), and the companion rule — a child
    ; named like a type in its parent is that type's method set — is what the
    ; nesting shows.

    ; `Write(p []byte) (n int, err error)`. IMPURE: it is the whole point.
    ;
    ; p IS AN ARRAY AND NOT A BUFFER, which is a claim read off the documented
    ; contract: "Implementations must not retain p", and Write does not modify it —
    ; it consumes the bytes. A buffer would say the host writes into it, which is
    ; `Read`'s claim and not this one.
    ;
    ; `n` is the count written, `0 ≤ n ≤ len p` by the contract; the range says the
    ; lower half, which is what the analysis can use, and `Write must return a
    ; non-nil error if it returns n < len(p)` is the caller's business.
    (module Writer
      (sig Write ((self go/io.Writer) (p (array (int 0 255)))) (tuple (int 0 9007199254740991) error)
        (host expr "%s.Write(%s)")))

    ; `Close() error`. Idempotence is NOT claimed: io.go says the behaviour of
    ; Close after the first call is undefined, which is a precondition no type can
    ; carry, so it is named here and not declared.
    (module Closer
      (sig Close ((self go/io.Closer)) error
        (host expr "%s.Close()")))

    ; `type WriteCloser interface { Writer; Closer }` — io.go's own definition, so
    ; the inclusion is the declaration rather than a summary of it.
    ;
    ; AND THE SUBTYPING IS DERIVED FROM IT. `T ≤ I` iff methods(I) ⊆ methods(T)
    ; (Cardelli's record subtyping with method sets, interfaces.md §2), and an
    ; inclusion is exactly that containment — so `(implements go/io.WriteCloser
    ; go/io.Writer)`, which this file carried until the companions existed, is now
    ; a theorem about these declarations.
    ;
    ; The included names stay ABSOLUTE: `include` takes module paths, and nesting
    ; abbreviates where a module is DECLARED, not where one is named.
    (module WriteCloser
      (include go/io/Writer go/io/Closer))))
